In Apache Kafka, Apache ZooKeeper and KRaft are used for cluster management and metadata coordination.
Understanding this difference is a very common interview question.

1️⃣ What is ZooKeeper in Kafka?

Earlier versions of Kafka used ZooKeeper to manage the Kafka cluster.

ZooKeeper is a distributed coordination system used to manage:

Broker metadata

Leader election

Cluster configuration

Topic information

Controller election

Architecture (Old Kafka)
Kafka Broker 1
Kafka Broker 2
Kafka Broker 3
       ↓
ZooKeeper Cluster

Kafka brokers communicate with ZooKeeper to get cluster information.

2️⃣ Example of ZooKeeper Responsibilities

ZooKeeper manages:

Broker registration

When a Kafka broker starts:

Broker → registers with ZooKeeper

ZooKeeper stores:

/brokers/ids/1
/brokers/ids/2
Leader election

Each partition has a leader.

If leader broker crashes:

ZooKeeper elects new leader
Metadata storage

ZooKeeper stores:

topics
partitions
replication info
cluster state
3️⃣ Problems with ZooKeeper

Using ZooKeeper created some operational challenges:

1️⃣ Extra system to maintain
2️⃣ More infrastructure complexity
3️⃣ Performance limitations
4️⃣ Harder cluster scaling

Because of this, Kafka introduced KRaft mode.

4️⃣ What is KRaft?

KRaft = Kafka Raft Metadata Mode

KRaft removes the dependency on ZooKeeper.

Kafka brokers manage metadata themselves using the Raft consensus protocol.

Architecture (New Kafka)
Kafka Broker 1
Kafka Broker 2
Kafka Broker 3
       ↓
KRaft Controller Quorum

No ZooKeeper required.

5️⃣ What KRaft Does

KRaft manages:

broker metadata

topic metadata

partition leadership

cluster configuration

All stored inside Kafka itself.

6️⃣ ZooKeeper vs KRaft
| Feature             | ZooKeeper Mode | KRaft Mode      |
| ------------------- | -------------- | --------------- |
| External dependency | Required       | Not required    |
| Complexity          | Higher         | Lower           |
| Performance         | Slower         | Faster          |
| Scalability         | Limited        | Better          |
| Future support      | Being removed  | Future of Kafka |

7️⃣ Interview Answer (Best Short Version)

If interviewer asks:

What is the difference between ZooKeeper and KRaft in Kafka?

Best answer:

Earlier Kafka versions used Apache ZooKeeper for cluster coordination, metadata storage, and leader election.
 Newer versions use KRaft (Kafka Raft Metadata mode), which removes the ZooKeeper dependency and manages metadata internally using the Raft consensus protocol.
  This simplifies architecture and improves scalability.

8️⃣ Extra Point (Good for 4-Year Experience)

You can add:

Kafka is gradually deprecating ZooKeeper mode, and modern Kafka clusters are moving toward KRaft-based architecture.